WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.553
Begin by framing Course Purpose and Faculty
Role as a question about inputs, decisions,

2
00:00:08.553 --> 00:00:10.375
and observable results.

3
00:00:10.375 --> 00:00:17.468
This is intended to allow faculty to pilot
potential new undergraduate offerings.

4
00:00:17.468 --> 00:00:24.372
Consider this worked case: A professor
proposes a new undergraduate course in

5
00:00:24.372 --> 00:00:30.125
theoretical computer science to test its
viability before full adoption.

6
00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:37.139
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

7
00:00:37.139 --> 00:00:37.640
conclusion.

8
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:43.546
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

9
00:00:43.658 --> 00:00:50.592
Now define Course Classification Clusters
precisely enough that another learner could

10
00:00:50.592 --> 00:00:52.483
test the definition.

11
00:00:52.483 --> 00:00:59.615
Specific course instances will be identified
as belonging to the Theory cluster, the

12
00:00:59.615 --> 00:01:02.358
Security cluster, or both clusters.

13
00:01:02.358 --> 00:01:09.031
Consider this worked case: A proposed course
on cryptographic protocols is classified under

14
00:01:09.031 --> 00:01:15.190
the Security cluster, while a course on
computational complexity falls under the

15
00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:16.217
Theory cluster.

16
00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:22.862
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

17
00:01:22.862 --> 00:01:25.279
evidence would distinguish them.

18
00:01:25.279 --> 00:01:31.920
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

19
00:01:32.042 --> 00:01:39.593
With the foundation in place, examine how
Faculty-Determined Details operates in a

20
00:01:39.593 --> 00:01:40.851
concrete sequence.

21
00:01:40.851 --> 00:01:45.711
Specific course details (such as
prerequisites, course seminar, format,

22
00:01:45.711 --> 00:01:52.192
learning outcomes, assessment methods, and
resource needs) will be determined by the

23
00:01:52.192 --> 00:01:58.132
faculty member(s) who propose a specific
seminar course in this area.

24
00:01:58.132 --> 00:02:04.523
Consider this worked case: The proposing
faculty member sets the prerequisite to

25
00:02:04.523 --> 00:02:10.913
include discrete mathematics and defines the
format as a three-hour weekly lecture.

26
00:02:10.913 --> 00:02:17.945
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

27
00:02:17.945 --> 00:02:23.741
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

28
00:02:23.858 --> 00:02:30.640
Next, compare the choices involved in Stable
Process Overview and make the decision

29
00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.683
criteria explicit.

30
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:35.995
This course examines current topics in Theory.

31
00:02:35.995 --> 00:02:42.941
Consider this worked case: The department
follows a standardized workflow: faculty

32
00:02:42.941 --> 00:02:49.886
submit a proposal, assign cluster(s), define
details, and pilot the course.

33
00:02:49.886 --> 00:02:56.984
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

34
00:02:56.984 --> 00:02:58.167
the goalposts.

35
00:02:58.167 --> 00:03:05.057
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

36
00:03:05.057 --> 00:03:11.969
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

37
00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:14.042
throughout the analysis.

38
00:03:14.162 --> 00:03:21.314
Apply Standards for Course Design to a worked
situation instead of treating it as an

39
00:03:21.314 --> 00:03:22.268
isolated definition.

40
00:03:22.268 --> 00:03:28.549
Hash tables map keys to locations and require
a collision strategy.

41
00:03:28.549 --> 00:03:35.510
Consider this worked case: The learning
outcomes specify that students will analyze

42
00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:41.891
algorithmic correctness and resource bounds,
assessed through a final theoretical proof

43
00:03:41.891 --> 00:03:42.471
assignment.

44
00:03:42.471 --> 00:03:50.190
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

45
00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:50.705
step.

46
00:03:50.705 --> 00:03:55.736
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

47
00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:03.236
Examine the tradeoffs in Planning Methods for
Resources, including what improves and what

48
00:04:03.236 --> 00:04:04.940
becomes more difficult.

49
00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:11.175
Asymptotic notation describes how resource use
grows with input size.

50
00:04:11.175 --> 00:04:18.683
Consider this worked case: The faculty member
lists required resources, including a

51
00:04:18.683 --> 00:04:24.940
whiteboard, projection system, and specific
textbooks on theoretical computer science.

52
00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:31.971
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

53
00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:39.206
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

54
00:04:39.314 --> 00:04:46.955
Stress-test the reasoning around Evaluation
Criteria for Proposals with an edge case and

55
00:04:46.955 --> 00:04:50.482
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

56
00:04:50.482 --> 00:04:55.170
Recurrences describe the cost of recursive
algorithms.

57
00:04:55.170 --> 00:05:02.170
Consider this worked case: The proposal is
evaluated based on whether it meets Theory

58
00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:08.170
cluster standards and whether the proposed
resources are sufficient for a successful

59
00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:08.670
pilot.

60
00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:15.013
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

61
00:05:15.013 --> 00:05:22.123
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

62
00:05:22.226 --> 00:05:30.217
Use Representative Examples of Theory Courses
to diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

63
00:05:30.217 --> 00:05:32.676
causes, and corrective action.

64
00:05:32.676 --> 00:05:40.308
OpenDSA presents data structures and
algorithms through executable visualizations

65
00:05:40.308 --> 00:05:42.004
and exercises.

66
00:05:42.004 --> 00:05:49.537
Consider this worked case: A sample Theory
cluster course covers loop invariants,

67
00:05:49.537 --> 00:05:52.676
divide-and-conquer algorithms, and graph
reachability.

68
00:05:52.676 --> 00:06:00.473
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

69
00:06:00.473 --> 00:06:02.144
smallest plausible cause.

70
00:06:02.144 --> 00:06:09.519
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

71
00:06:09.626 --> 00:06:16.485
Connect Application to Course Planning to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

72
00:06:16.485 --> 00:06:17.857
as one system.

73
00:06:17.857 --> 00:06:24.044
Dynamic programming records solutions to
overlapping subproblems.

74
00:06:24.044 --> 00:06:31.470
Consider this worked case: The faculty member
reviews the proposal against the evaluation

75
00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:37.754
criteria, makes necessary adjustments, and
submits the final plan for departmental

76
00:06:37.754 --> 00:06:38.325
approval.

77
00:06:38.326 --> 00:06:47.968
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

78
00:06:47.968 --> 00:06:49.575
across representations.

79
00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:57.294
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

80
00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:03.911
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

81
00:07:03.911 --> 00:07:04.375
analysis.

82
00:07:04.375 --> 00:07:11.889
Algorithm analysis connects a precise problem
specification to a correctness argument and

83
00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:13.141
resource bounds.

84
00:07:13.141 --> 00:07:20.726
Consider this worked case: The checklist
includes items like 'Proposal submitted',

85
00:07:20.726 --> 00:07:26.242
'Cluster assigned', 'Details defined',
'Resources secured', 'Evaluation criteria

86
00:07:26.242 --> 00:07:29.000
met', and 'Pilot approved'.

87
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:37.044
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

88
00:07:37.044 --> 00:07:38.281
verification step.

89
00:07:38.281 --> 00:07:45.106
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

90
00:07:45.106 --> 00:07:45.594
intuition.
